## D. Sequential testing on ASOS

ASOS metrics accumulate per user (means grow and variances roughly double over a test), so information is not
proportional to n. All sequential analyses use the relative lift with **variance-based information**,
I_k = 1/Var(relative lift). The largest gap between n-based and variance-based fractions has median
{{ PK.median_max_gap_n_vs_variance_information|f(2) }} per trajectory.

**Model-based null** (Brownian information-time model on the real look schedules; OBF ≈ 5% holds by construction):
naive daily peeking {{ NS.naive.fpr|pct }}, OBF {{ NS.obf.fpr|pct(2) }}, mSPRT {{ NS.msprt.fpr|pct(2) }}, one look
{{ NS.fixed_horizon.fpr|pct(2) }}. mSPRT is conservative because Ville's bound is built for unlimited looks; it wastes
α with about {{ A.null_peeking_simulation.median_looks|f(0) }} looks.

![peeking](../results/figures/peeking.png)

**Real trajectories vs the same model** ({{ PK.n_trajectories }} trajectories, {{ PK.n_final_significant }} significant at the end):

