"Final does not support" is not the same as "false positive". In the model, about
{{ MB.npmle_effects.unsupported_but_true_effect_same_sign|f(0) }} of the {{ MB.npmle_effects.unsupported|f(0) }} unsupported naive
calls are real effects (|lift| > 0.1%, right sign) that the final test failed to confirm. The interesting result is the
excess: the real portfolio crosses and reverses far more often than the model allows. That is consistent with
non-Brownian dynamics (novelty, weekly cycles, telemetry spikes), and it means the real naive error rate is
plausibly above the model's {{ NS.naive.fpr|pct }}. OBF had {{ PK.obf.strong_reversals_final_significant_opposite }} strong reversals.

**Stopping time, compared fairly.** On the {{ PK.stopping_on_common_set.n_detected_by_both_msprt_and_obf }} trajectories that
both mSPRT and OBF detect, the median stop is day {{ PK.stopping_on_common_set.median_stop_day_msprt|g }} (mSPRT) vs day
{{ PK.stopping_on_common_set.median_stop_day_obf|g }} (OBF), with a median horizon of {{ PK.median_horizon_days|g }} days. mSPRT stops a
little earlier on the effects it catches, but it catches far fewer.

